{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>The mouth of the righteous buds forth wisdom, and a froward tongue shall be cut off (Proverbs 10:31).</b>" "<b>Solomon</b> the king, peace be upon him, informs us in this verse (Proverbs 10) that the righteous man and the wicked man are opposites, and the speech of the righteous is the fruit of wisdom while the speech of the wicked is the contrary thereof. The righteous is likened to a tree, as it is written (Psalms 1:3): ״והיה כעץ שתול על פלגי מים אשר פריו יתן בעתו ועלהו לא יבול״ — and a tree puts forth leaf and fruit, and this matter is natural in it, for so was it implanted in it on the third day by the utterance (Genesis 1:11): ״תדשא הארץ״. The leaf has utility, for it shields the fruit in its shade from the heat of the sun, and the fruit is the principal — so too the righteous has leaf and fruit: his leaf is his speech in mundane matters, in the affairs of this world, in his disciplines and the recounting of his affairs, and there is great benefit in them just as there is benefit in the leaf that shields in its shade; and this is what the Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: ״ועלהו לא יבול״ — even the casual talk of Torah scholars requires study. His fruit is his speech in Torah and in wisdom, which is the principal, just as the fruit is principal in the tree. And because of this Solomon mentioned here ״פי צדיק ינוב חכמה,״ explaining that the speech of the righteous is the fruit of wisdom. And this is what they said: ינוב, from the language of (Isaiah 27:6) ״ומלאו פני תבל תנובה,״ (Lamentations 4:9) ״מתנובות שדי,״ and from this matter (Isaiah 57:19) ״בורא ניב שפתים,״ for speech is the fruit of the lips. And it comes to say that the righteous man so habituates himself to speak wisdom that wisdom becomes natural in him, like this fruit which is the principal of the tree. And ״ולשון תהפוכות תכרת״ — he called the wicked man ״a froward tongue,״ for he is the opposite of the righteous, for his measure in all the words of his mouth is contrary to the measure of the righteous whose mouth buds forth wisdom; and because of the great disgrace of this trait in the wicked, he attached to it a curse and said ״תכרת״ — shall be cut off. For so is the way of Torah: to attach blessing to the mention of the righteous and curse to the mention of the wicked, as it says (Proverbs 10): ״זכר צדיק לברכה ושם רשעים ירקב.״ And it is known that the trait of humility falls within the category of wisdom, and the wicked, whom he called a froward tongue, possesses the trait of pride, for it is its opposite in all things; and just as wisdom is the fruit of the righteous, so pride is the fruit of the wicked. And this is what Solomon said (ibid. 14): ״בפי אויל חטר גאוה ושפתי חכמים תשמורם״ — the root of pride is in the heart of the fool and the branch is in his mouth, in the manner of (Isaiah 11:1) ״ויצא חטר מגזע ישי,״ and a branch cannot but bear fruit, and the fruit is words of pride and contempt, in the manner of (Psalms 123:4) ״הלעג השאננים הבוז לגאיונים.״ And it comes to say that the fool so habituates himself in the trait of pride, in his mouth and in his heart, that pride becomes natural in him; for since the branch of pride is in his mouth, it is a clear thing that it will produce fruit, for the fool cannot guard himself from this — but the wise, their lips guard them from it. And we learn from all this that the trait of the wicked is entirely the opposite of the trait of the righteous in every detail of his affairs. And the rationale behind all this: the righteous is drawn after the affairs of the soul, which is eternal, and the wicked is drawn after the affairs of the body, which perishes. And it is a known thing that the body is from the earth and the soul is from the heavens; and behold, while a person is in this world in life, he has nearness in the heavens and in the earth — in the heavens from the side of the soul, and in the earth from the side of the body. Therefore, when Moses admonished Israel concerning the fulfillment of Torah and commandments, he summoned as witnesses against them the heavens and the earth, which are a person's kin, like a person who warns his fellow and testifies against him with witnesses in the presence of his kin. And this is what he said:" "<b>Give ear, O heavens, and I will speak; and let the earth hear the words of my mouth.</b> Moses addressed the heavens directly and the earth covertly. The reason for this is that he was drawing near to the heavens and departing from the earth, for this was on the day of his death. And so with all the matters, all of them: beginning with ״אתם נצבים״ in which he assembled all Israel and said to them ״אתם נצבים היום,״ and also the portion ״וילך״ in which he mentioned בן מאה ועשרים שנה אנכי היום, and also this portion in which is written ״בעצם היום הזה עלה אל הר העברים,״ and also in the portion ״וזאת הברכה״ which is written concerning the day before his death — all on one day, and it is the day of his death. And the portions, all of them, involve a joining together of many great and wondrous matters, worthy of being performed over many days, and they were all done by way of miracle on a single day." "<b>And know</b> that the general content of this portion is that Moses was reproving Israel and declaring to them from beginning to end and all the events that will come to pass in the future. And he began from the creation of the world and concluded with the matter of the days of the Messiah. This is what he said: ״זכור ימות עולם וגו׳, בהנחל עליון גוים״ — that is, that the Most High caused the nations to inherit and separated them into their tongues; He did not choose for His portion any of them but Israel, as it says ״כי חלק ה׳ עמו.״ ״כי מצאם בדרך המדבר מקום תהו ויללת תנין ובני יענה, יסובבנהו יבוננהו יצרנהו כאישון עינו״ — all these kindnesses He did with them, and they forgot Him and provoked Him; this is what he said ״צור ילדך תשי, יקניאוהו בזרים בתועבות יכעיסוהו.״ And they were ungrateful, becoming estranged from Him on account of abundant goodness and tranquility, in the manner of what is written ״וישמן ישרון ויבעט.״ They would abandon the service of the Master who did them good and would serve one who did not do them good and in whom they knew no power of divinity; this is what is written ״אלהים אשר לא ידעום.״ And because of this they were punished with the punishment of exile more than any nation and were condemned to serve their enemies. And the reason He did not obliterate their remembrance from the world, as they deserved by the measure of justice, was lest the enemies boast and say that their god had conquered and prevailed. This is what is written ״פן ינכרו צרירנו״ — for they are not possessors of understanding to consider how one of them pursued a thousand and two put ten thousand to flight of Israel, were it not that their Rock had sold them, had delivered them into the hand of the nations because they forsook His Torah. And yet He is destined to take vengeance upon the wicked for having subjugated Israel and to pay them according to their deed; this is ״לי נקם ושלם.״ And the Holy One, blessed be He, is destined to take up His case against them; this is ״כי ידין ה׳ עמו,״ and He will remember for them the measure of mercy, and from this He calls them His people and His servants — for until now they had been servants of Ishmael and Kedar. And He said: ״ראו עתה כי אני אני הוא״ — for until here were the words of Moses, and now the Holy One, blessed be He, will speak, and He Himself will guarantee the promise of the future redemption, and He swears upon it: ״כי אשא אל שמים ידי, אם שנותי ברק חרבי״ — that is, that I will sharpen the lightning of My sword, and it is in the future tense in the manner of prophecies. And He said: ״הרנינו גוים עמו״ — for all the nations will affirm us and praise us and wish to cleave to our faith after they see the vengeance He will exact upon His foes. And He said ״וכפר אדמתו עמו״ — that is, that He will atone for the land and for His people who dwell upon it." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> ״האזינו השמים״ — the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses: Say to Israel, Look at the heavens that I created to serve you — perhaps they changed their measure? Or perhaps the wheel of the sun does not rise from the east and illuminate the entire world, in the manner of what is written (Ecclesiastes 1:5) ״וזרח השמש ובא השמש״? Look at the earth — perhaps it changed its measure? You sowed in it and it did not sprout; you sowed wheat and it produced barley? Or perhaps it said: O cow, I will not plow today and I will not thresh; and O donkey, I will not load and I will not go? Now this is all the more so (קל וחומר): if these, which if they merit receive no reward and if they sin receive no punishment, did not change their measure — then you, who if you merit will receive reward and if you sin will receive punishment, how much the more so! The meaning of this Midrash is that all natural things do not change from their habit and do not depart from the line designated and fixed for them. And the reason He mentioned the heavens and the earth is that they maintain their stature and their measure and their nature, and likewise the sea, which keeps its boundary and does not depart from its measure — for if it were to depart from the line of sand that is its boundary, it would destroy the world. And likewise the cow and the donkey, which are living creatures — their activity is according to their nature and does not change at all. And since natural things do not change their measure and their nature, how much more so for human beings, possessors of intellect and of reward and punishment, who must not change their measure or depart from the line of divine service and Torah and commandment." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: האזינו השמים ותשמע הארץ — Tiferet and Kavod, in the mystery of the first verse. For with these heavens and earth we enter into covenant, and therefore Moses says that the conditions between us shall be heard. And he mentioned the language of דבור (speaking) with regard to the heavens and the language of אמירה (saying) with regard to the earth, to explain that every דבור is from the heavens and every אמירה is from the earth, and what he spoke with the earth was in hiddenness, because of fear of the kingship, and this is clear."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:53:34.502184673Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}